        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Does Walmart Sell Treadmills? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Yes. Walmart sells treadmills, but mostly on Walmart.com, and many of the
            treadmill listings there come from third-party Marketplace sellers rather than from
            Walmart itself.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Walmart.com lists named-brand machines such as NordicTrack and ProForm, the two big
            consumer brands owned by iFIT, alongside walking pads and folding treadmills from
            dozens of brands most buyers will never have heard of. Some of those listings are sold
            and shipped by Walmart. Many are not. On a Marketplace listing the seller is a separate
            company, and under Walmart's own policy that seller is responsible for shipping,
            returns and customer service, including return shipping costs.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the first thing to check on any Walmart treadmill is the line on the product page
            that says who sells and ships it. "Sold and shipped by Walmart.com" means Walmart's
            standard return policy applies and Walmart is your point of contact. Anything else needs
            a closer look before you buy. The rest of this page explains why, and how the answer
            changes the return window, the warranty and how easy it will be to get help if the
            machine arrives damaged.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walmart can be a perfectly sensible place to buy a treadmill. Its pricing on mainstream
            machines is competitive, its return policy for items it sells itself is long, and it has
            stores almost everywhere for small returns. It is a less sensible place to browse,
            because the listing quality varies so widely that a good machine and a poor one can sit
            side by side at similar prices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-sells-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Sold by Walmart or a Marketplace Seller: Why It Matters</h2>
<p>
            Walmart.com works in a similar way to Amazon. Walmart sells some products itself and lets
            approved outside companies sell others through the same website. For a treadmill there
            are three arrangements you will see, and the product page tells you which one you are
            looking at.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">1. Sold and shipped by Walmart</h3>
          <p>
            Walmart is the retailer. Walmart's standard return policy applies, which on 18 October
            2026 gave most items 90 days. Walmart handles the order, the delivery and the return, and
            the manufacturer handles the warranty. This is the simplest arrangement and the one most
            buyers assume they are getting.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2. Sold by a seller, fulfilled by Walmart</h3>
          <p>
            Some Marketplace sellers store their stock in Walmart's warehouses through a programme
            called Walmart Fulfillment Services. The seller owns the product and sets the price, but
            Walmart packs and ships it. Walmart's own seller documentation says Walmart handles the
            return process for these orders. The listing still names the outside seller, and the
            return window shown on the listing is the one to trust.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. Sold and shipped by a Marketplace seller</h3>
          <p>
            The outside company sells, stores and ships the treadmill, and Walmart's website is the
            shop window. Walmart's return policy says that Marketplace sellers "are responsible for
            processing, shipping, returns, and customer service" for their orders, "including return
            shipping costs". It also says most Marketplace items are returnable within 30 days, that
            some are not eligible for return to a Walmart store, and that sellers' own policies may
            include restocking fees of up to 20%.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why this is the main risk.</strong> With a heavy machine, the difference between
            those three arrangements is the difference between dropping a return off with a large
            retailer and negotiating freight collection with a company you have never heard of,
            possibly paying for it, possibly losing a fifth of the price. It also affects the
            warranty: a seller that is not an authorised dealer for the brand may not give you a
            machine the manufacturer will support, and some Marketplace treadmills come from brands
            with no US support presence you can find.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this means Marketplace listings are bad. Plenty are from established brands
            selling through their own storefronts. It means you need to know which kind you are
            buying, and read that seller's terms rather than Walmart's. Our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            covers the same problem on the other big marketplace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-walmart-sells" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Which Treadmill Brands Walmart Carries</h2>
<p>
            Because Walmart.com blocked our automated checks, we could not count its treadmill
            listings directly. Search-engine copies of Walmart.com pages, read on 18 October 2026,
            and independent buying guides point to the same broad picture.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>iFIT brands sold by Walmart.</strong> Walmart.com has brand pages for NordicTrack
            and ProForm, and NordicTrack treadmill listings appeared in search results marked "Sold
            and shipped by Walmart.com", including folding T Series machines and the larger
            Commercial range. These are mainstream machines also sold direct and through other
            retailers, which makes them the easiest Walmart treadmills to compare on price. Most are
            designed around iFIT's paid training subscription, so check what the machine can do
            without it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Other named brands.</strong> Listings from established budget and mid-range
            brands such as Sunny Health &amp; Fitness and XTERRA also appear on Walmart.com, some
            sold by Walmart and some by Marketplace sellers. Check the seller line on each listing
            rather than assuming from the brand.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A long tail of Marketplace brands.</strong> A large share of Walmart.com's treadmill
            results are walking pads, "2-in-1" under-desk machines and compact folding
            treadmills from little-known brands, often sold and shipped by Marketplace sellers.
            Some are reasonable walking machines. Many have thin specification sheets, motor ratings
            that are not stated as continuous, and warranty terms that are hard to find.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The house-brand history.</strong> Walmart has long been associated with
            budget treadmills made by ICON Health &amp; Fitness, the company now called iFIT. For
            years the Gold's Gym treadmills sold at Walmart and other mass retailers were ICON
            machines made under licence; that arrangement ended in 2019, as our
            <a href="/golds-gym-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Gold's Gym treadmill guide</a>
            explains. Weslo, another ICON budget brand, was also a familiar mass-retail name. We
            could not confirm that Walmart lists new Weslo treadmills today, so treat an old review
            of a Weslo or Gold's Gym model as history rather than a current option, and check that
            any listing under those names is new stock with a working warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="in-store-online" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. In Store vs Online at Walmart</h2>
<p>
            Most Walmart treadmill shopping happens on the website or in the app, and it is worth
            setting expectations before you drive to a store.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>In store.</strong> Walmart stores sell fitness equipment, but floor space is
            limited, and where treadmills are stocked they are more likely to be boxed walking pads or
            compact folding machines than full-size running treadmills set up to try. Stock varies
            from store to store. The app shows whether an item is in stock at your chosen store
            for pickup; check that, or call the store, before making a trip. Even if a store has a
            machine in a box, you will usually not be able to walk on it first.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Online.</strong> Walmart.com carries the full range, including larger machines
            that are shipped to your home and Marketplace listings that never pass through a Walmart
            building. Online is also where the seller question from the previous section applies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pickup.</strong> Some items can be ordered online and collected in store. For a
            boxed treadmill that means you need a vehicle that will take the box and enough people to
            lift it. A compact folding treadmill in its box can weigh well over 100 pounds, and a
            full-size machine considerably more; our guide to
            <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-weigh/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill weighs</a>
            gives typical boxed weights by type.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price matching.</strong> Walmart's price-match policy, read on 18 October 2026,
            says US stores will match the price of an identical item advertised on Walmart.com.
            It does not match competitors' prices, Marketplace sellers' prices, or Walmart.com
            special-event prices such as Rollbacks and Black Friday deals. If you want to try a
            treadmill in person before buying, a
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-treadmill-near-me/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">specialty or sporting goods store near you</a>
            is the better place to do it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-a-listing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How to Check a Walmart Treadmill Listing</h2>
<p>
            A few minutes on the product page saves most of the problems buyers report. Work through
            it in this order.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Seller and shipper.</strong> Find the line that says who sells and ships the item. If it is not Walmart.com, click through to the seller's profile and read its return policy and contact details.</li>
            <li><strong>Brand and model number.</strong> Can you find the same model number on the manufacturer's own website? If the brand has no website, no US phone number and no manual you can download, assume support will be hard to get.</li>
            <li><strong>Continuous motor rating.</strong> A "3.5 HP" headline is often a peak figure. Look for continuous horsepower; our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> explains why it matters.</li>
            <li><strong>Running surface.</strong> Belt width and length, stated as two separate numbers. Walking needs less than running; see our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> for what your stride needs.</li>
            <li><strong>Maximum user weight</strong>, and whether the specification sheet agrees with the title. On marketplace listings the two do not always match.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty.</strong> Frame, motor, parts and labour, stated separately, and who honours it.</li>
            <li><strong>Return details.</strong> The return window and conditions shown on this listing, not the general policy.</li>
            <li><strong>Delivery.</strong> What the delivery includes and whether assembly is offered.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Treat reviews with care.</strong> Reviews can be pooled across variants and
            sellers, and a treadmill that has only been used for a few weeks has not had time to show
            its weaknesses. Look for reviews that mention months of use, motor or belt problems, and
            how the seller handled a fault.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking pads are not small treadmills.</strong> Many of the cheapest Walmart
            listings are walking pads with no handrail and a low top speed. They suit walking at a
            desk, not running. Our
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs treadmill comparison</a>
            explains the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Walmart's Return Policy for Treadmills and Heavy Items</h2>
<p>
            We read Walmart's US return policy on 18 October 2026. It was marked "Updated: October 9,
            2026". These are the parts that matter for a treadmill. Policies change, so check the
            current version and the return details on your listing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The standard window is 90 days.</strong> The policy gives "90 days after purchase
            or upon receipt" to return or exchange an item "unless noted in our exceptions". The
            exceptions list did not name treadmills or fitness equipment when we read it. It did give
            shorter windows to several categories, including consumer electronics at 30 days, so a
            connected machine's listing is worth checking for its own terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Most Marketplace items get 30 days.</strong> For items shipped by Marketplace
            sellers, the policy says "most items are returnable within 30 days", that the seller's
            own policy governs refunds, and that restocking fees of up to 20% are possible. It also
            recommends insuring returns of items valued above $600 before shipping them back.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heavy items do not go back to the store.</strong> The policy says
            "freight/oversized/heavy items are not returnable to stores", and that items requiring
            assembly must be fully disassembled before being returned to a store. If you ship an
            item back from home, Walmart asks that it be securely repackaged. For a treadmill that
            means taking it apart and keeping the box until you are sure, because a return may mean
            boxing it up again for a carrier.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Holiday purchases.</strong> The policy says most items bought between October 1
            and December 31 can be returned until January 31, with some exceptions. That can extend
            the window for a treadmill bought as a gift, but read the listing for exclusions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walmart+ and returns.</strong> The policy says Walmart+ members have "additional
            home pickup options" for returns. Whether a home pickup covers a freight-delivered
            treadmill is something to confirm with Walmart before you rely on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not treat the window as a trial.</strong> A 90-day return on a heavy machine is
            useful insurance, but every return of a treadmill involves dismantling, packing and
            arranging collection. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a>
            compares how retailers handle this and what to check before you need it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery-assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Delivery, Assembly and Walmart+</h2>
<p>
            How a Walmart treadmill reaches you depends on its size and on who is shipping it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Smaller machines</strong> such as walking pads usually arrive by parcel carrier,
            left at the door like any other large box.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Larger treadmills</strong> are more likely to come by freight, sometimes with a
            delivery appointment. Read what the delivery includes before you buy. Curbside, threshold
            and room-of-choice delivery are very different services when the box weighs as much as
            a person, and a Marketplace seller's freight terms can differ from Walmart's. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a>
            explains the levels and what to ask.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Assembly.</strong> Walmart.com lists paid treadmill assembly services that can be
            added to an order, priced by the value of the treadmill and carried out by a third-party
            home-services provider. Check whether the service is available at your address and for
            your specific machine, and that it is booked for after delivery. If you would rather do it
            yourself, our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembling a treadmill</a>
            sets out the job.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walmart+.</strong> Walmart+ is Walmart's paid membership. Its benefits centre on
            free delivery from stores, shipping and extra return options, and they rarely change which
            treadmill you should buy. If you are not already a member, do not join only for one
            treadmill unless you have checked that the membership changes the delivery or return
            terms for that particular listing, and add the fee to the price when you compare.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Measure first.</strong> Check the route from the door to the room and the space
            the machine needs when it is running, including clearance behind the deck, not just its
            footprint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Warranty, Protection Plans and Recalls</h2>
<p>
            Walmart sells the machine; the manufacturer usually provides the warranty. That is true
            whether Walmart or a Marketplace seller sold it, but the seller can affect how easily you
            can use it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Find the warranty before you buy.</strong> A mainstream brand publishes separate
            terms for frame, motor, parts and labour. A budget Marketplace listing may state a short
            warranty, a vague one or none, and the contact for claims may be the seller rather than
            a manufacturer with parts and technicians. Register the machine if the maker asks you to,
            and keep the order confirmation, model and serial numbers together.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Protection plans.</strong> Walmart offers protection plans at checkout, and the
            return policy gives Walmart Protection Plans a 30-day return window. Before buying one,
            compare what it covers with the manufacturer's warranty you already get. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains when extended cover earns its price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check for recalls.</strong> Recalls apply to a product wherever it was sold. A
            2022 recall of two Nautilus treadmills over a fall hazard, for example, listed Walmart
            and Best Buy among the retailers. Before buying, and occasionally after, search the
            brand on the Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall site, saferproducts.gov.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Walmart vs Amazon, Costco, Best Buy and Home Depot</h2>
<p>
            Walmart competes with the other big general retailers on price and convenience. Here is
            how it compares for a treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Amazon</strong> is the closest comparison: an enormous range, mostly from
            third-party sellers, with the same need to check who is selling and the same difficulty
            returning a heavy item. Walmart's advantage is a long return window on items it sells
            itself and stores for smaller returns.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Costco</strong> carries a much smaller, rotating range and requires a membership,
            but sells only what it chooses to stock and has a strong return reputation. Our
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-at-costco/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Costco guide</a>
            explains how to judge its model numbers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Best Buy</strong> leans towards connected, screen-led machines and open-box
            units. See our <a href="/best-buy-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Best Buy guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Home Depot</strong> lists treadmills online, but when we checked, its range was
            made up of little-known budget brands rather than the names Walmart carries. Our
            <a href="/home-depot-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Home Depot treadmill guide</a>
            sets out what it does and does not offer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wayfair</strong> also mixes supplier brands and house labels, with its own return
            and delivery terms; see <a href="/wayfair-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Wayfair</a>.
            <strong>Sporting goods and specialty stores</strong> let you try a machine and often
            include delivery, assembly and local service, usually at higher prices.
          </p>
          <p>
            The fair comparison is always the delivered price for an equivalent specification, with
            the return terms and warranty alongside. Our
            <a href="/treadmills-for-sale/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for sale guide</a>
            sets out what each channel usually includes, and the
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy</a>
            covers when prices tend to fall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Walmart Suits, and Who It Does Not</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Walmart suits</strong> buyers who already know which mainstream model they want
            and find it sold and shipped by Walmart at a good delivered price; buyers who value a long
            return window on items Walmart sells itself; and walkers looking for a basic walking pad
            or folding machine who are willing to check the seller and warranty carefully.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It suits less well</strong> runners who want to try a long deck and a strong
            motor before buying; heavier users who need a high, credible weight capacity;
            anyone who wants delivery to an upstairs room with assembly handled by the same company;
            and buyers who do not want to sort through Marketplace listings of uncertain quality.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And it is not the place to buy on price alone.</strong> The cheapest treadmill
            listings on any marketplace are where specifications are vaguest and support is hardest
            to find. If a full-size treadmill costs far less than every comparable machine from a
            known brand, the difference is usually in the motor, the frame, the warranty or the
            seller.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Before You Buy a Treadmill at Walmart</h2>
<p>
            Run through these before you place the order.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Seller.</strong> Sold and shipped by Walmart.com, fulfilled by Walmart, or sold and shipped by a Marketplace seller?</li>
            <li><strong>Return window.</strong> What does this listing say, and who would collect the machine?</li>
            <li><strong>Restocking fee.</strong> Does the seller's policy charge one?</li>
            <li><strong>Brand support.</strong> Does the maker have a website, a manual and a US support contact for this model?</li>
            <li><strong>Specification.</strong> Continuous horsepower, running surface, maximum user weight, and does the spec sheet match the title?</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty.</strong> Frame, motor, parts and labour, and who honours it?</li>
            <li><strong>Delivery and assembly.</strong> Which delivery level, and is an assembly service available for this item at your address?</li>
            <li><strong>Recalls.</strong> Has the brand had a treadmill recall on saferproducts.gov?</li>
            <li><strong>Delivered price compared.</strong> Against the same or an equivalent machine elsewhere, including any membership you would join for it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If any answer is "I don't know", ask the seller before you order, and treat a seller
            that cannot answer as a reason to buy elsewhere.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Walmart sell treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Walmart sells treadmills mainly on Walmart.com and in its app, including NordicTrack and ProForm machines and many walking pads and folding treadmills. Many listings are from third-party Marketplace sellers rather than Walmart itself. Stores may stock a few boxed machines, but range and stock vary by location.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you return a treadmill to Walmart?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, but how depends on the seller. When we read Walmart's policy on 18 October 2026, most items sold by Walmart had 90 days and most Marketplace items had 30 days. Freight, oversized and heavy items cannot be returned to stores, and items needing assembly must be disassembled first. Check the return details on your listing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does "sold and shipped by Walmart.com" mean on a treadmill listing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It means Walmart is the retailer, so Walmart's own return policy applies and Walmart handles the order and the return. If a listing names another seller, that seller's terms apply instead, and Walmart's policy says Marketplace sellers handle shipping, returns and customer service, including return shipping costs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Walmart have treadmills in store?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some stores stock a small number of boxed treadmills or walking pads, but most of the range is online and you will rarely be able to try one. Check in-store availability for your chosen store in the Walmart app, or call the store, before making a trip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Walmart assemble treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Walmart.com lists paid treadmill assembly services that can be added to an order, priced by the value of the machine and carried out by a third-party provider. Check that the service is available at your address and for your machine before relying on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Walmart treadmills good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some are mainstream machines also sold elsewhere, and some are budget Marketplace listings with thin specifications and short warranties. Judge each one on continuous horsepower, running surface, maximum user weight, warranty and who is selling it, not on the store or the price alone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Buying a Treadmill at Walmart</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of them come from assuming that everything on Walmart.com is sold by Walmart.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking who the seller is</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A Marketplace seller sets its own return terms, may charge a restocking fee and handles its own customer service. Read the seller line on the product page and that seller's policy before you order, not after the box arrives.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning to take it back to the store</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Walmart's policy says freight, oversized and heavy items are not returnable to stores, and items needing assembly must be taken apart first. Find out how a return would work for this machine and keep the box until you are sure.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a walking pad for running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many of the cheapest listings are walking pads with low top speeds and short decks. They suit walking at a desk, not running. Check the top speed, the running surface and whether there is a handrail.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a headline motor figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Marketplace titles often quote peak horsepower and generous weight limits that the specification sheet does not support. Look for a continuous motor rating and check the spec sheet against the title and the manufacturer's own figures.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

